---
import HagueDoc from '../../../layouts/HagueDoc.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Can I renounce a China inheritance from Canada?',
    a: 'Yes. You typically need a notarized, apostilled waiver for the notary office or real-estate registry in China. The wording has to be right the first time: which estate, whether it covers representation, whether it is irrevocable. Call 647-930-6688.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How is a waiver different from a Power of Attorney?',
    a: 'A waiver means you are no longer an heir. A POA means you still inherit, but someone else signs and files for you. The two documents are not interchangeable. Miao He confirms which one the China notary office actually asked for.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if the wording is wrong?',
    a: 'Changing it after the Apostille means starting over. China notary offices are strict about the word “renounce.” Recent waivers Miao He drafted were accepted on the first pass, with urgent originals the next day.',
  },
];
---
<HagueDoc lang="en" slug="renunciation-of-inheritance" faqs={faqs}>
  <div class="definition-box">
    <p>Miao He recently apostilled a <strong>renunciation of inheritance</strong> so a China estate could proceed. An heir in Canada had to issue the waiver. The wording was accepted on the first pass — no supplementary materials, no rewrite — and the urgent original was available the next day.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>When you need it</h2>
  <p>There is property or a bank account in China. Other heirs need a notarial inheritance certificate or a title transfer. You are in Canada and you do not want to inherit. The notary office in China will usually require an apostilled waiver before it will issue a certificate to the remaining heirs.</p>
  <ul class="content-list">
    <li>you do not want the China property, and the tax or co-ownership that comes with it;</li>
    <li>the other heirs need your waiver to register in their names alone;</li>
    <li>the notary office’s list expressly requires a waiver from the heir abroad.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Miao He is a dual-licensed litigation lawyer. She knows how China inheritance notaries test the word “renounce.” The declaration states the estate, the relationship to the deceased, and whether the waiver is irrevocable. Vague language is rejected.</p>

  <h2>What to bring</h2>
  <ul class="content-list">
    <li>your passport and proof of kinship (a <a href="/en/hague-apostille/parent-child-declaration/">parent-child declaration</a> if needed);</li>
    <li>the death certificate and the China notary office or lawyer’s document list;</li>
    <li>the title certificate or other estate inventory, if available.</li>
  </ul>
</HagueDoc>
